      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The relationships among troponin concentration, early phase coagulation activation and soluble P-selectin concentration was evaluated in this study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "METHODS AND RESULTS",
          "content": "Troponin-l, soluble P-selectin, von Willebrand factor (vWF), fibrinogen, plasminogen, plasminogen activator inhibitor 1 (PAI-1) and tissue plasminogen activator (t-PA) concentrations of 202 consecutive patients with non-ST elevation acute coronary syndrome (NSTE-ACS) were measured at the time of admission. Patients were classified into 2 groups as troponin-negative (<0.2 ng/ml, n=129) or positive (>or=0.2 ng/ml, n=73). Soluble P-selectin concentrations were found to be higher (p<0.001) and correlated with troponin concentrations (r=0.313, p<0.005) in troponin-positive patients with NSTE-ACS. In these patients fibrinogen (p<0.001), plasminogen (p<0.001) and PAI-1 (p<0.026) concentrations were higher and t-PA concentrations were lower (p<0.013) and all these parameters correlated with P-selectin concentrations (p<0.001). There was no difference between the groups according to vWF concentrations (p=0.379). Soluble P-selectin concentrations were found to be an independent predictor of troponin positivity (beta=0.295, odds ratio =1.05, p<0.001). Analysis of regression revealed a significant effect of troponin on soluble P-selectin concentrations (r=0.52, r2=0.27, p<0.001)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The results suggest that higher soluble P-selectin concentrations might be involved in increased coagulation activation and myocardial injury development in patients with NSTE-ACS."
        }
      ]},
